{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,260p' '_meta/agents/Kourovka/_common-kourovka.md' && printf '\\\\nSCOPE_RECORD\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' 'Agents/Kourovka/scopes/21.137-odd-prime-exponent-p2.json' && printf '\\\\nSAFE_BRIEF\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' 'Agents/Kourovka/scopes/briefs/21.137-odd-prime-exponent-p2.md' && printf '\\\\nINBOX_FILES\\\\n' && rg --files 'Agents/Kourovka/bus/inbox/Problem-21.137' 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,260p' '_meta/agents/Kourovka/_common-kourovka.md' && printf '\\\\nSCOPE_RECORD\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' 'Agents/Kourovka/scopes/21.137-odd-prime-exponent-p2.json' && printf '\\\\nSAFE_BRIEF\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' 'Agents/Kourovka/scopes/briefs/21.137-odd-prime-exponent-p2.md' && printf '\\\\nINBOX_FILES\\\\n' && rg --files 'Agents/Kourovka/bus/inbox/Problem-21.137' 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"/mnt/c/users/kmuli/downloads/math-agents-main/math-agents-main/obsidian\n---\nname: kourovka-common\ndescription: \"Shared protocol for every agent in the Kourovka crew. Defines the Obsidian file bus, the time-budget contract, the compute budget, write scopes, authorship, and the stop conditions. Every Kourovka role prompt inherits this file. Read it first, every session.\"\napplies_to: [Lead, Validator, MathExpert, Problem-NN.MM]\nprotocol_version: 2\n---\n\n# Kourovka crew — common protocol\n\nRead this file **first, in full, every session**, before your role file.\nIf your role file and this file conflict, **this file wins** on protocol; your role\nfile wins on role-specific judgement.\n\n---\n\n## 0. What this program is\n\nFifty Kourovka Notebook open problems, worked by a crew of AI agents, with the goal\nof producing **a real solution, counterexample, or reduction** on as many as\npossible. Some problems have already been solved outside this crew, so the bar is\n\"did we actually close it\", not \"did we write a nice report\". Any solution reserved\nas a sealed benchmark remains outside the vault under §12.\n\nThe single most likely failure mode for this program is **an agent convincing itself\nit has solved an open problem when it has not.** Every rule below exists to make\nthat failure loud and early instead of quiet and late. A 47-problem \"no result,\nhonestly reported\" run is a success. One false claim shipped to a human as a proof\nis a total failure of the whole crew.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fixed paths\n\n**Every path below is vault-relative.** Your working directory *is* the vault root —\nit was set with `codex -C` or Codex was launched from there. Write `Agents/Kourovka/...`,\nnever a leading `/mnt/c/...`. Machine-specific absolute paths do not appear anywhere\nin this program's prompts, and you must not introduce one.\n\nThis program runs on **Windows**, but the crew lives in a Linux shell (WSL2/Ubuntu,\nor Git Bash). So: POSIX paths with forward slashes, never `C:\\Users\\...`. If you\never need to hand a path to a Windows program, convert it with `wslpath -w`; do not\nhand-write one.\n\n| What                        | Path (relative to vault root)                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Problem corpus (JSONL)      | `Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/corpus/kourovka-21-corpus.jsonl`  |\n| Problem corpus (readable)   | `Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/corpus/kourovka-issue-NN-YYYY.md` |\n| Corpus overview + caveats   | `Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/_kourovka-21-corpus.md`           |\n| Problem syntheses (the 50)  | `Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/<id>-<slug>.md`                   |\n| **All inter-agent comms**   | `Agents/Kourovka/`                                                              |\n| Canonical scope records     | `Agents/Kourovka/scopes/<scope-key>.json`                                       |\n| Reusable method playbooks   | `Agents/Kourovka/methods/`                                                       |\n| Benchmark run records       | `Agents/Kourovka/benchmarks/`                                                    |\n| Solved-problem write-ups    | `Experiments/Kourovka/<id>-<slug>/`                                             |\n| Scratch / compute workspace | `Agents/Kourovka/problems/<id>/scratch/`                                        |\n| Machine-local path config   | `_meta/agents/Kourovka/paths.env`                                               |\n\n### `paths.env` — the source PDF, and the clock\n\nTwo things are not vault-relative: the Kourovka Notebook PDF, which lives outside\nthe vault at a location that differs per machine, and UTC timestamp arithmetic,\nwhose syntax differs per shell. Both are resolved through\n**`_meta/agents/Kourovka/paths.env`** — the single place in this program where a\nmachine-specific value is allowed to exist. Source it, then use what it gives you:\n\n```bash\nsource \"_meta/agents/Kourovka/paths.env\"\n\npdftotext -f <page> -l <page> -layout \"$KOUROVKA_PDF\" -   # read the real statement\nkv_now                                                     # 2026-08-11T14:03:00Z\nkv_deadline 3                                              # that instant + 3 hours\n```\n\n| Name | Is |\n|---|---|\n| `$KOUROVKA_PDF` | the Notebook PDF |\n| `$KOUROVKA_PAPERS` | the directory holding it inside the vault |\n| `$KOUROVKA_VAULT` | the vault root |\n| `$KOUROVKA_AUTHOR` | explicitly named human operator; required before research-note writes or agent spawns |\n| `kv_now` | current UTC, ISO-8601 |\n| `kv_deadline <hours>` | UTC now + n hours, ISO-8601 |\n\n**Never write a bare `date` for a deadline.** `date -d` and `date -v` mean different\nthings in different shells; `kv_deadline` picks the right one. A deadline computed\nby hand is how the §3 timing contract quietly breaks.\n\nIf `$KOUROVKA_PDF` doesn't resolve, that is a `BLOCKER` for Lead — do not guess at a\npath, and do not fall back to the corpus text (§7 forbids it).\nIf `$KOUROVKA_AUTHOR` is empty or a placeholder, that is a `BLOCKER` before spawn.\nNever infer it from a login, filesystem path, or git metadata.\n\nNever write outside the vault. **Nothing in this program writes into the repo that\nholds the PDF**. It is kept inside the vault so agents can read it without granting\nan additional writable directory to Codex.\nNo commits. Nobody in this crew commits anything, ever. The human handles git.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Obsidian file bus\n\nAgents communicate **only by writing and reading files under `Agents/Kourovka/`.**\nThere is no canvas, no messaging CLI, no shared terminal, no socket. If you find\nyourself looking for a tool to send a message with, there isn't one — you write a\nfile.\n\nThis is deliberate: every exchange in the program is on disk, timestamped, and\nauditable after the fact. Nothing that influenced a decision is lost when a terminal\ncloses.\n\n### 2.1 Layout\n\n```\nAgents/Kourovka/\n├── board/\n│   ├── _board.md              # Lead-owned human-readable scheduling view.\n│   └── _decisions.md          # Lead-owned. Append-only decision log (extend/skip/kill).\n├── scopes/                    # Lead-owned machine-readable records, one per atomic target scope.\n├── methods/                   # Reusable, problem-independent research playbooks.\n├── benchmarks/                # Public run manifests only; never private benchmark solutions.\n├── roster/\n│   └── <AgentName>.md         # Lead-owned. One per live agent: session id, problem, budget.\n├── bus/\n│   ├── inbox/\n│   │   ├── Lead/              # anyone may write here\n│   │   ├── Validator/         # anyone may write here\n│   │   ├── MathExpert/        # anyone may write here\n│   │   └── <Problem-NN.MM>/   # created by Lead when it spawns that agent\n│   └── archive/               # processed messages are MOVED here, never deleted\n└── problems/\n    └── <id>/                  # e.g. 11.48/\n        ├── log.md             # the problem agent's append-only working log\n        ├── findings.md        # the problem agent's current best claim + evidence\n        └── scratch/           # code, GAP scripts, data. Not read by other agents.\n```\n\n### 2.2 Message format\n\nA message is **one file**, named:\n\n```\nAgents/Kourovka/bus/inbox/<Recipient>/<UTC-ISO8601>__<Sender>__<TYPE>__<slug>.md\ne.g.  2026-08-11T1430Z__Problem-11.48__CLAIM__seventh-Engel-word-not-fifth-power.md\n```\n\nBody — this exact frontmatter, then free text:\n\n```markdown\n---\nfrom: <Sender>\nto: <Recipient>\ntype: REQUEST | REPORT | CLAIM | QUESTION | BLOCKER | VERDICT | IDEA | CHECKPOINT | DECISION | CORRECTION | SPAWN | KILL | PING\ntopic: <one line>\nproblem: <NN.MM or \"none\">\nscope_id: <NN.MM/name or \"none\">\nassignment_revision: <positive integer or \"none\">\nsupersedes: [<message filenames or \"none\">]\nrefs: [\"[[note-a]]\", \"Agents/Kourovka/problems/11.48/findings.md\"]\nneeds_reply_by: <UTC timestamp or \"none\">\nstatus: unread\n---\n\n## Ask\n<Exactly what you want the recipient to do. One sentence. If FYI, write \"FYI, no action.\">\n\n## Context\n<What they need to know to act. Link, don't paste.>\n\n## Evidence\n<Commands run, outputs, file paths. Only for CLAIM / REPORT / VERDICT.>\n```\n\n### 2.3 Reading your inbox\n\n- **Poll, don't wait.** At the start of every working cycle: list your inbox dir,\n  sort by filename (they sort chronologically), read everything with\n  `status: unread`.\n- **Process oldest first**, except `type: BLOCKER` and `type: KILL`, which jump the\n  queue.\n- When you have acted on a message: set `status: done` in its frontmatter, then\n  **move the file** to `bus/archive/`. Never delete a message.\n- If you cannot act on it, set `status: blocked` and add a `## Blocked because`\n  section. Leave it in the inbox and send a `BLOCKER` to Lead.\n\n### 2.4 Rules\n\n- **Write only into someone's inbox.** Never edit a file inside another agent's\n  `problems/<id>/` directory or their roster entry.\n- **One message, one ask.** If you have three asks, write three messages.\n- **Link, never paste.** Messages reference notes; they do not duplicate content.\n  A message over ~40 lines is a note that should have been written somewhere else\n  and linked.\n- **No message means no answer.** There is no synchronous channel. If you need\n  something, you write a file; then you continue with other work, or you park.\n- **Scope and revision control authority.** For problem work, the canonical target\n  is the `scope_id` and `assignment_revision` in `Agents/Kourovka/scopes/`. A\n  `CORRECTION` from Lead on a newer revision supersedes older `KILL`, `VERDICT`, and\n  scheduling messages for the older revision. Old evidence remains valid only for\n  the scope it actually addresses; old control instructions do not govern a newer\n  assignment.\n- **Human instructions outrank the state machine.** Lead converts a human rescope or\n  correction into an atomic new scope revision before work continues. Agents do not\n  silently reinterpret the instruction, and they do not keep obeying stale control\n  messages merely because those messages are still in an inbox.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Timing contract\n\nThis is a hard, load-bearing part of the program. Do not improvise around it.\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Cycle** | One 3-hour block of work by a problem agent on one atomic scope and direction. |\n| **Initial budget** | Every activated scope gets exactly **one 3-hour cycle** to start. |\n| **Extension** | Lead may grant **+1 hour at a time**, only on evidence of promise. There is no multi-hour extension. |\n| **Review circle** | Problem agent → Validator → Math Expert → Lead. All four steps. |\n\n### 3.1 Budget accounting\n\nThe budget measures **active research time**, not calendar delay. Count reasoning,\nwriting, supervised computation, and inspection of output. Record but do not charge\ntime spent waiting for another agent, waiting for a compute slot, or while the\nprocess is not running. Each work start/stop entry records UTC and cumulative active\nminutes; Lead audits the latter against the cycle budget.\n\nRules:\n\n1. A problem agent works its cycle under active Lead supervision. It sends a\n   `CHECKPOINT` at approximately 60 active minutes, and earlier when a strategy hits\n   its kill criterion, a scope mismatch appears, or a representation-changing pivot\n   is needed. Routine logging stays local; checkpoints contain decisions, not diary\n   entries.\n2. **At 3 hours the agent must produce exactly one outcome from §3.2.** A\n   strategy outcome is not automatically a verdict on the scope or notebook\n   problem.\n3. Lead never presents a crew-generated solution or counterexample claim to the\n   human before a full review circle. This restriction does **not** prohibit\n   operational questions, scope clarification, resource approval, portfolio\n   choices, or periodic progress digests. Those may go to the human whenever Lead\n   cannot safely decide them within already approved authority.\n4. On `STRATEGY_LIVE`, Lead may grant **+1 hour** when the next experiment is\n   concrete, bounded, and worth its portfolio cost. After that hour, the same\n   outcome vocabulary applies. Lead logs every extension in\n   `board/_decisions.md` with the evidence that justified it.\n5. On `STRATEGY_EXHAUSTED`, Lead selects a materially different strategy, switches\n   direction, consults MathExpert, or parks the scope under the safeguards in the\n   Lead protocol. Exhausting one method never retires the scope by itself.\n6. **Extension cap:** no scope gets more than **+4 hours** cumulative across both\n   directions without the\n   human explicitly approving more. At +4, Lead escalates to the human with the\n   evidence and a recommendation.\n7. Time is measured by the agent recording UTC start/stop and cumulative active\n   minutes in `log.md`. An agent that reports a \"3-hour cycle\" after 20 active\n   minutes has broken the contract — Lead treats the report as void.\n8. At approximately **30 active minutes**, the problem agent performs a short\n   self-check: target scope, current hypothesis, evidence, and whether the chosen\n   representation is still productive. At approximately **60 active minutes**, it\n   writes `## One-hour checkpoint` and sends the decision summary to Lead. The\n   checkpoint names: target scope; new facts; current strategy; what was ruled out;\n   bottleneck; two qualitatively different alternatives; recommended next\n\nSCOPE_RECORD\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"protocol_version\": 2,\n  \"scope_key\": \"21.137-odd-prime-exponent-p2\",\n  \"problem_id\": \"21.137\",\n  \"scope_id\": \"21.137/odd-prime-exponent-p2\",\n  \"assignment_revision\": 2,\n  \"source\": {\n    \"issue\": 21,\n    \"pdf_page\": 184,\n    \"statement_checked\": true,\n    \"checked_by\": \"Lead\",\n    \"checked_utc\": \"2026-08-16T11:13:07Z\",\n    \"transcription_note\": \"Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/21.137-power-set-subgroup-powerful.md\",\n    \"independent_scope_audit\": {\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"passed\",\n      \"auditor\": \"Validator\",\n      \"note\": \"Agents/Kourovka/problems/21.137/verification/2026-08-16T114327Z-source-fidelity-audit.md\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"target_statement\": \"Let p be an odd prime and G a finite p-group of exponent exactly p^2. If the set P={g^p : g in G} of p-th powers is a subgroup of G, then P is abelian.\",\n  \"source_clauses\": [\n    {\n      \"clause_id\": \"c-general\",\n      \"text\": \"If the p-th powers in a finite p-group form a subgroup, must that subgroup be powerful?\",\n      \"in_scope\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"clause_id\": \"c-odd\",\n      \"text\": \"For p not equal to 2, if the p-th powers in a p-group of exponent p^2 form a subgroup, must that subgroup be abelian?\",\n      \"in_scope\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"clause_id\": \"c-two\",\n      \"text\": \"For a 2-group of exponent 8, if the squares form a subgroup, must that subgroup be abelian?\",\n      \"in_scope\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"constraints\": [\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-forall-p-G\",\n      \"kind\": \"quantifier\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"The assertion quantifies over every prime p and group G satisfying all remaining admissibility rows.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-p-not-2\",\n      \"kind\": \"exclusion\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"p is prime and p is not 2; equivalently p>2.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-finite-p-group\",\n      \"kind\": \"object_class\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"G is a finite p-group for that same odd prime p.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-exponent-p2\",\n      \"kind\": \"exponent\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"The exponent of G is exactly p^2.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-power-set-definition\",\n      \"kind\": \"definition\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"P is the set of all actual p-th powers {g^p : g in G}, not merely the subgroup generated by those powers.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-power-set-subgroup\",\n      \"kind\": \"hypothesis\",\n      \"role\": \"admissibility\",\n      \"statement\": \"The set P of actual p-th powers is closed as a subgroup of G.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"constraint_id\": \"21.137-odd-P-abelian\",\n      \"kind\": \"conclusion\",\n      \"role\": \"target_conclusion\",\n      \"statement\": \"P is abelian.\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_clause_id\": \"c-odd\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"excluded_scopes\": [\n    \"21.137/two-group-exponent-8\",\n    \"Any p=2 example\",\n    \"Any odd-prime group whose exponent is not exactly p^2\"\n  ],\n  \"success_criteria\": {\n    \"proof\": \"Prove P abelian for every odd prime p and every finite p-group G of exponent exactly p^2 whose actual p-th-power set P is a subgroup.\",\n    \"counterexample\": \"Give a reconstructible finite p-group for an explicitly odd prime p, of exponent exactly p^2, whose actual p-th-power set is a subgroup but is nonabelian.\",\n    \"partial_progress\": [\n      \"A proved reduction from closure of the p-th-power set to explicit commutator identities.\",\n      \"A complete, reproducible exclusion or construction result for a structurally defined family at an odd prime.\"\n    ],\n    \"certificate\": \"A line-by-line proof, or a presentation and independent checks of p>2, exact exponent p^2, equality with the actual power set, subgroup closure, and nonabelianity.\"\n  },\n  \"selection_status\": \"approved\",\n  \"state\": \"reconnaissance\",\n  \"scope_answered\": false,\n  \"active_direction\": \"proof\",\n  \"truth_likelihood\": {\n    \"estimate\": 0.58,\n    \"rationale\": \"Planning estimate only: odd-prime power-commutator identities make a positive reduction plausible, but closure of a word-value set is weak enough that counterexamples remain credible.\",\n    \"updated_utc\": \"2026-08-16T11:13:07Z\"\n  },\n  \"priority\": {\n    \"importance\": 4,\n    \"probability_of_substantial_progress\": 0.4,\n    \"verifiability\": 5,\n    \"expected_active_hours\": 3.0,\n    \"rationale\": \"The clause is exact, parameter-sensitive, and supports both algebraic reductions and independently checkable finite models.\"\n  },\n  \"direction_sessions\": {\n    \"proof\": {\n      \"session_id\": null,\n      \"state\": \"not_started\",\n      \"parent_session_id\": \"01a00064-840f-7150-b101-e01ea04d1660\",\n      \"fresh_session_reason\": \"The old session was anchored to an excluded p=2 clause and solution-bearing context; the human requested a blind odd-prime restart.\"\n    },\n    \"counterexample\": {\n      \"session_id\": null,\n      \"state\": \"not_started\",\n      \"parent_session_id\": null,\n      \"fresh_session_reason\": null\n    }\n  },\n  \"current_strategy\": \"odd-prime Hall-Petrescu and commutator-map reduction\",\n  \"strategy_history\": [\n    \"The legacy p=2 direction is excluded from this scope and from the clean-context prompt.\"\n  ],\n  \"latest_outcome\": \"human-authorized clean odd-prime restart pending scope audit\",\n  \"open_questions\": [],\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"changed_utc\": \"2026-08-14T13:09:58Z\",\n      \"reason\": \"Legacy whole-problem assignment; no canonical atomic record existed.\",\n      \"supersedes\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"revision\": 2,\n      \"changed_utc\": \"2026-08-16T11:13:07Z\",\n      \"reason\": \"Atomize the odd-prime exponent-p^2 clause and exclude the separate exponent-8 two-group clause.\",\n      \"supersedes\": [\n        \"legacy whole-problem assignment\",\n        \"all revision-1 control messages\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"blind_run\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"open_web\": false,\n    \"solution_bearing_history_allowed\": false,\n    \"claim_of_clean_pretraining\": false\n  },\n  \"updated_utc\": \"2026-08-16T11:13:07Z\"\n}\n\nSAFE_BRIEF\n---\ntitle: \"Clean-context brief: 21.137 odd-prime exponent-p-squared clause\"\nauthor: operator\ntags: [agent/lead, user/operator, domain/group-theory, topic/kourovka, topic/p-groups, project/kourovka, status/draft]\n---\n\n# Active statement\n\nLet (p>2) be prime and (G) a finite (p)-group of exponent exactly (p^2).\nIf the set (P=\\{g^p:g\\in G\\}) of actual (p)-th powers is a subgroup, must\n(P) be abelian?\n\n# Non-negotiable exclusions\n\nThe exponent-8 two-group clause is a separate scope. A (p=2) example is out of\nscope. A group of exponent other than exactly (p^2), or a check involving only\nthe generated verbal subgroup (G^p) without proving equality with the actual\npower set, is also out of scope.\n\n# Direction and first strategy\n\nThe planning estimate is 0.58 that the assertion is true, so this run starts in the\nproof direction. Translate closure of the actual (p)-power set into exact\nHall--Petrescu/commutator identities and look for a bilinear, quadratic, Lie-ring,\nor class-reduction formulation. Any candidate must be checked row-by-row against\nthe canonical constraints before it is treated as relevant.\n\n# Clean-context boundary\n\nUse only the source PDF, canonical scope, this brief, and local mathematical tools.\nDo not inspect the ordinary synthesis, prior run artifacts, verification notes,\narchived messages, or shared-chat material. Do not search the web for a solution.\nNo claim is made that model pretraining is contamination-free.\n\nINBOX_FILES\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
